From c5dd96a31e542e1a062453ae7b32d4cee90f89e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 09:24:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CREATE OBJECT with a dynamic name checks the static type of
 the target

`CREATE OBJECT ref TYPE (lv_name)` only checked that the name resolves to a
class at all. Whatever it resolved to was then assigned to the reference, so a
class unrelated to the reference's static type went in without a word and the
mismatch surfaced later as a raw javascript TypeError on the first member
access - which no CATCH takes. A caller that guards its dynamic CREATE OBJECT
exactly the way the ABAP documentation asks for was therefore not guarded at
all, and a system raises a catchable CX_SY_CREATE_OBJECT_ERROR for the same
statement.

Found in abap2UI5, where it is reachable from outside: the framework starts an
app with `CREATE OBJECT li_app TYPE (app_start)` into a `REF TO z2ui5_if_app`,
`app_start` comes from the request URL, and the two CATCH blocks around it
turn a bad name into a 500 with a message. On a system a name pointing at a
class that is not an app lands in those blocks; transpiled, the whole request
died with no body, no status code and none of its headers.

Checked on the CLASS, not on an instance, so the constructor does not run
first - that is what a system does, and it keeps the check free of side
effects. A `REF TO object` target has nothing to check against and emits no
check, so nothing changes for the generic case that every existing dynamic
test covers.

The runtime predicate is the one the cast already uses: `instanceof` for a
class target, the IMPLEMENTED_INTERFACES list for an interface target.

Three tests: an incompatible class, a class that does not implement the
target interface, and one that does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
